<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brunei People's Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1956">1956</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Parti Rakyat Brunei</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a> People's Party was a political party founded in 1956 under the leadership of A. M. Azahari, calling for independence, democracy and the union of Brunei, Sarawak and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">North Borneo</a> under the sultan. It won 54 of the 55 district council seats in the 1962 elections, and with them all 16 elected seats of Brunei's Legislative Council, but in December 1962 its armed wing launched the Brunei revolt, which British forces quickly suppressed. The party was then banned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brunei-peoples-party.jpg" alt="Brunei People's Party" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NorthTension · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_PRB.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a>'s nationalist movement is usually dated to an underground campaign led by Muhammad bin Haji Manggol in Temburong District in October 1953, which the government put down. Inspired by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesian-national-revolution">Indonesian National Revolution</a>, A. M. Azahari, with the help of Salleh Masri, founded the Parti Rakyat Brunei on 21 or 22 January 1956 as an offshoot of Ahmad Boestamam's Malayan People's Party. It was the country's first officially recognised political party, but was registered on 15 August only after breaking with its Malayan parent and confining itself to Brunei.</p><p>The party was left-wing and anti-colonial. It sought a sovereign, democratic Malay nation under the monarchy, a Unitary State of North Kalimantan joining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">North Borneo</a> and Sarawak, and a Legislative Council three-quarters elected. Most of its members were non-aristocratic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malays">Malays</a>, and by 1957 it had 16,000 members.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A party delegation met the Secretary of State for the Colonies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 5 September 1957 but was told to deal first with the sultan, a setback that cost it money and public confidence. Azahari denounced the Constitution of 1959 as inadequate, and the party opposed the plan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunku-abdul-rahman">Tunku Abdul Rahman</a>, put forward on 27 May 1961, for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a> to join a Federation of Malaysia. Brunei's first district council election, held on 30 August 1962 amid the talks on Malaysia, gave it an overwhelming victory, which its leaders read as popular support for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">North Borneo</a> state in place of Malaysia.</p><p>Its armed wing, the North Kalimantan National Army, had been formed in August 1960 at the house of Azahari's father in Kampong Padang, with Azahari's brother Sheikh Othman as its brigadier general, and some recruits trained with the Indonesian National Army. Azahari claimed about 30,000 members. At 2:00 a.m. on 8 December 1962 it rose against the government; the sultan declared a state of emergency, British troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> ended the rising within days, and 2,063 members were arrested.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Azahari found refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> under President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> and set up a government in exile. On 12 July 1973 Zaini Ahmad, Yassin Affandi and other detainees escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limbang">Limbang</a> in Sarawak, and on 7 May 1974 the party was reactivated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuala-lumpur">Kuala Lumpur</a> with Azahari as president, lobbying foreign governments for representative government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a>. Yassin co-founded the National Development Party in 2005, and after the deaths of Azahari, Zaini and Yassin the party is considered defunct.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Brunei People's Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In December 1962 a revolt led by the <b>Brunei People's Party</b> was suppressed with British help, and in 1963 the sultan declined to join Malaysia, keeping control of Brunei's oil wealth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brunei-sultanate">Brunei Sultanate · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
